		<article id="intersection-safety-systems-prototyping-awards-announcement" class="newsletter-article">
			<h2 class="newsletter-article-title text-center">Intersection Safety Systems (ISS)<br />Prototyping Awards Announcement</h2>

			<figure class="newsletter-image">
				<img
					src="https://content.govdelivery.com/attachments/fancy_images/USDOTFHWA/2026/10/14620307/7213986/intersection-safety-systems-logo-fullcolor-3x_crop.png"
					alt="Intersection Safety Systems"
				/>
			</figure>

			<div class="newsletter-article-content">
				<p>
					The U.S. Department of Transportation (USDOT) is pleased to announce
					the teams selected for the ISS Prototyping Broad Agency
					Announcement (BAA). Building on the success of the USDOT
					Intersection Safety Challenge, the ISS Prototyping initiative aims
					to advance innovative ISS that can identify, predict, and mitigate
					unsafe conditions involving vehicles and road users at
					intersections in real-time and under real-world conditions. Full
					end-to-end prototype systems are expected to leverage emerging
					technologies, including sensor fusion, super intelligence-based
					decision-making, and conflict mitigation to enhance intersection
					safety.
				</p>
				<p>
					The ISS BAA received 23 innovative proposals. Recipients were selected
					through a collaborative process led by USDOT, bringing together
					modal experts from the Office of the Assistant Secretary for
					Research and Technology, Intelligent Transportation Systems Joint
					Program Office (ITS JPO), Federal Highway Administration, National
					Highway Traffic Safety Administration, Federal Transit
					Administration, and Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration.
				</p>
				<p>The three teams selected are:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Derq USA, Inc.</li>
					<li>University of California, Los Angeles</li>
					<li>University of Michigan Transportation Research Institute</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					USDOT awarded a total of $5.5M for Phase 1 ISS prototype development and
					testing in access-controlled roadway intersection testbeds. If
					promising progress is demonstrated in Phase 1, an additional $11.5M
					is expected to be available for Phase 2 prototype refinement and
					assessment in real-world intersections.
				</p>
				<p>Congratulations to the awardees!</p>

				<figure class="newsletter-image">
					<img
						src="https://content.govdelivery.com/attachments/fancy_images/USDOTFHWA/2026/10/14604532/7209150/issbaafeaturepic_crop.jpg"
						alt="Traffic signals and associated sensors from the Intersection Safety Challenge Stage 1B"
					/>
					<figcaption>Source: USDOT</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<p><em>This image depicts traffic signals and associated sensors captured during the Intersection Safety Challenge Stage 1B.</em></p>

				<div class="newsletter-cta-wrapper align-right">
					<a href="https://www.its.dot.gov/research-areas/intersection-safety/systems/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="newsletter-cta">
						<strong>Learn More About ISS Prototyping</strong>
					</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</article>

